{
  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Potentially: the Manual says a plant variety name combined with other matter can be distinctive, depending on the additional material; this is not a prediction that your particular mark would be accepted.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/3/2-3-1-a-variety-names",
          "quote": "However, a plant variety name in combination with other matter can be distinctive depending on the additional material in the mark."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The starting difficulty is that the Manual treats the variety name itself as the name of the product, even if it was invented or coined.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/3/2-3-1-a-variety-names",
          "quote": "Although cultivated plant variety names are invented or coined, they are nevertheless a part of the scientific nomenclature. The name given to a plant variety is the name of the product. It is the apt name when referring to that particular plant."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Section 41 requires rejection for lack of capacity to distinguish, but provides that a mark lacks that capacity only if subsection (3) or (4) applies.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s41",
          "quote": "(1) An application for the registration of a trade mark must be rejected if the trade mark is not capable of distinguishing the applicant’s goods or services in respect of which the trade mark is sought to be registered (the designated goods or services) from the goods or services of other persons."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s41",
          "quote": "(2) A trade mark is taken not to be capable of distinguishing the designated goods or services from the goods or services of other persons only if either subsection (3) or (4) applies to the trade mark."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Simply substituting another marketing name for the accepted variety name does not resolve distinctiveness: the Manual treats that substitute as an alternate or de facto variety name.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/3/2-3-1-c-trade-names-and-alternate-names~1",
          "quote": "In summary, if someone chooses to apply another name to a plant variety and does not use its apt name, then the other name becomes an alternate or de facto name for that variety since there is no other way of identifying it. In this case, the alternate name is not distinctive and is not prima facie inherently adapted to distinguish as a trade mark."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual's practice is to distinguish the trade mark clearly from the variety name, rather than use it instead of that name.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/3/2-3-1-c-trade-names-and-alternate-names~1",
          "quote": "However, both agree that trade marks in use need to be clearly differentiated from the variety name and not used instead of it."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/3/2-3-1-c-trade-names-and-alternate-names~1",
          "quote": "Acceptable formats for trade mark purposes are where the trade designation precedes the genus/variety information or is otherwise clearly separated from it."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Separately, section 43 requires rejection where a connotation of the mark or a sign within it would make its use on the specified goods or services likely to deceive or cause confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s43",
          "quote": "An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "For rose variety names, the Manual identifies a section 43 concern where the mark connotes one variety but is used for a different variety of the same or a closely related genus.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/4/2/4/1",
          "quote": "If a trade mark contains or consists of material that connotes a particular plant variety, it would be deceptive or confusing if the trade mark was applied to goods which are a different variety of the same or closely related genus."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/4/2/4/1",
          "quote": "In such a circumstance, a ground for rejection under section 43 is applicable."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "That assessment must consider the mark as a whole and whether the variety connotation is immediate, rather than treating every inclusion of a variety name alike.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/4/2/4/1",
          "quote": "Section 43 requires that the connotation is immediate."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/4/2/4/1",
          "quote": "However, the examiner needs to consider how the variety name appears within the trade mark as a whole. For example, if the plant name is a dictionary word appearing in a phrase then connotation may not be immediate."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For a plant-name objection under section 41, the Manual allows for possible acceptance with a condition where declaratory evidence demonstrates trade mark use alongside genus, species and variety information.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/3/2-3-5-b-evidence-of-use",
          "quote": "The Applicant could provide evidence in declaratory form to overcome a ground for rejection under sections 41(3) or 41(4)."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/3/2-3-5-b-evidence-of-use",
          "quote": "While it is unlikely that evidence could overcome the problem in the conventional sense of examination, particularly in relation to s41(3), it may be possible to accept the trade mark with a condition of registration if the evidence demonstrates use of a trade mark in conjunction with genus, species and variety name information."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For section 43, the Manual also records possible conditional acceptance where examples show use in close conjunction with named varieties of the relevant plant type.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/4/2-4-3-b-examples-of-use",
          "quote": "The Applicant could provide examples of use to overcome a ground for rejection under section 43. If the examples show that the trade mark is being used in close conjunction with named varieties of the problem plant type, it may be possible to accept the trade mark with a condition of registration."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Consent from a Plant Breeder's Rights title holder is not, by itself, an answer to a section 41 or section 43 objection under the Manual.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part32A/2/1/2-1-2-c-letter-of-consent",
          "quote": "Note: In instances where an application has received a ground for rejection under more than one section of trade mark legislation, a Letter of Consent will not overcome a ground for rejection under sections 41 or 43."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    }
  ],
  "declined": false,
  "concepts_used": [
    "GC-0036",
    "GC-0072",
